{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310716,"nonce":"BoTg8FMFCVhEuyjZ9+QEh4BGnXI=","title":"EIGHT distinct defects behind the stdlib silent wrong answers, separated by MECHANISM. D1 FIXED for scalar/String in 1fb6a169 (Array.map ...","message":"`Array.map`, `htmlEscape`, `urlEncode` returning garbage are **eight separate\ndefects**, not one. All verified against Haxe 4.3.6, all deterministic (66 cold\nruns, zero variance). Harness: `scratchpad/STDLIB_REGRESSION/run.sh`\n(`REPS=3 bash run.sh`) — 22 cases incl. controls, checks the LIVE oracle every\nrun, `rm -rf .rayzor` + `--no-cache` before each, exits non-zero on regression.\n\n\n**`Array.map` is a symptom, not the bug.** Pure Haxe, no Array method, no native\ntrampoline, still wrong:\n\n```haxe\nstatic function hof(a:Array\u003cString\u003e, f:Dynamic-\u003eString):String return f(a[0]);\nhof([\"a\"], x -\u003e x + \"!\")   // oracle r=a!   rayzor r=\u003cunknown type 72220674\u003e!\n```\n\nTwo independent causes; either alone produces it:\n- The formal becomes `Dynamic`: `ast_lowering/resolve.rs:456-458` matches only\n  `TypeKind::Class`, so an `Array` receiver (`TypeKind::Array`) gets no hint;\n  `infer.rs:904/924/943` build map/filter/sort callbacks as `dynamic_type()`\n  rather than `Function`; `expr/calls.rs:899` is the ONLY push of\n  `expected_lambda_params_stack`, so var-decl/field initialisers and\n  `return \u003clambda\u003e` are structurally uncovered.\n- **Nothing boxes at the call**: `expr/calls.rs:600-616` recovers formals only\n  for NAMED callees (`Ident` -\u003e function symbol, or `Field` on a class), so an\n  indirect call returns None and `calls.rs:921` skips coercion. This half is why\n  an EXPLICIT `(x:Dynamic)` annotation fails identically — no hint plumbing can\n  reach it.\n\nSilent for strings (garbage inside the returned String), SIGSEGV for `filter` /\nfield access. `[1,2,3].map(x -\u003e x + 10)` is CORRECT — the selectivity is why it\nshipped. Workaround: bind to a typed local.\n\n\nTwo edits, committed 1fb6a169. Conformance 431 -\u003e 433 (Issue2244, Issue2378), nothing lost:\n- `ir/mir/expr/call/indirect.rs` — `lower_indirect_call` now boxes an argument\n  into a `Dynamic` formal via `maybe_box_value`, reading the formals off the\n  callee's `Function` type it already extracted for the signature. **Restricted\n  to Int/Float/Bool/String arg kinds** — see below for why.\n- `tast/ast_lowering/resolve.rs` — the instance-call arm of\n  `resolve_callee_formal_param_types` gains an `Array { element_type }` receiver\n  case returning `Function([T], ..)` for `map`/`filter`/`sort` ONLY, so the\n  untyped lambda's param is typed to the element. Other Array methods still\n  answer None (no new argument coercion); a `TypeParameter` element stays\n  `Dynamic` (generic-erasure family).\n\nHarness: g1a (Array.map strings), g1e (pure HOF), g1g (filter SIGSEGV) -\u003e FIXED;\nIssue2244 WRONG_ANSWER -\u003e PASS. g1d (`var f:String-\u003eString = x -\u003e ..`) **FIXED 6552dc9d**: the `var`/`final` arms\nnow push the declared `Function` type's params as the lambda hint around the\ninitializer. Still broken, expected: g1b (explicit `(x:Dynamic)` — the lambda\nreally unboxes, the native trampoline passes raw; no hint can reach it).\n\n**THE FINDING THAT BOUNDS THIS: the `Dynamic` convention is NOT uniform on the\ncallee side.** A `Dynamic` parameter is UNBOXED when consumed as a String\n(`haxe_std_string_ptr`) or scalar, but used as a RAW pointer when it is a\nfunction, anonymous, class or enum. Proof: the first version boxed every kind\n(as the direct-call path does) and turned `Issue6480` — which passes a function\nvalue and an anon into an IIFE's `Dynamic` params and then CALLS the function —\nfrom a 26/30-pass flake into a **deterministic SIGBUS** (5/5), and moved\n`Issue2580` (enum into a local fn's `Dynamic` param) CRASH -\u003e WRONG_ANSWER.\nNarrowing to scalar+String restored both (Issue6480 PASS 3/3). So \"box\neverything at the boundary\" cannot be copied from the direct path to the\nindirect one; a complete D1 fix has to make the callee side unbox uniformly\nfirst. Attributed by 5-rep mini-harness on the indirect-only binary BEFORE\nrebuilding — the binary cannot be preserved by copying (haxe-std is exe-relative).\n\n\nSame mechanism as [[bugs-cross-module-new-dropped-by-compile-order]], which\nde609a53 fixed for USER modules only. Breaks `StringTools.keyValueIterator`\n(silently returns 0 items), contributes to `htmlEscape`, `Array.iterator` and\n`StringTools.iterator` lower to `ret $0`.\nWidening de609a53's gates to stdlib was MEASURED INERT (both gates lifted; nothing changed). The fix was ordering — see D2 STATUS below (23e37cbd, cb5ee997).\n\n\n`D2` was TWO ordering defects, found in sequence:\n\n1. **Discovery parsed with the wrong defines** (`compilation.rs`, fixed 23e37cbd).\n   `load_imports_efficiently` extracted dependencies from\n   `parser::parse_haxe_file(.., false)` = the parser's DEFAULT config\n   (`rayzor`,`sys`,`debug`) — no `eval`. The compile defines `eval`\n   (`preprocessor_config()`), so every `#if eval` body vanished before\n   dependency extraction: StringMap/IntMap/ObjectMap construct\n   `haxe.iterators.MapKeyValueIterator` only inside `#if eval`, so no edge, so\n   never ordered first — and never COMPILED at all. Blast radius inventoried:\n   +3 edges (the three maps), 2 unresolvable refs skipped (`eval.Vector`,\n   `sys.ssl.Socket`), 0 edges lost (the apparent `!eval` losses were my\n   scanner not closing single-line `#if .. inline #end`).\n   Exposed a second defect the moment the module was reached: `@:nullSafety(Off)`\n   → `Cannot find name 'Off'` — metadata ARGS were lowered as expressions.\n   Fixed e1b7dc96 (`expr/mod.rs` Meta arm lowers params permissively; HIR ignores\n   them anyway).\n\n2. **Cycle tie-break emitted the constructor's caller first** (fixed cb5ee997).\n   Stuck files were emitted by `min(outstanding_deps, name)`; alphabetically\n   `haxe.ds.IntMap` \u003c `haxe.iterators.MapKeyValueIterator` and `Array` \u003c\n   `haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator`, so the class that CONSTRUCTS its iterator\n   always lowered before the iterator existed → value wrap. Discovery now also\n   records what each file CONSTRUCTS (a `new:`-tagged marker partitioned into a\n   side map), and the victim score is `(outstanding, builds_a_stuck_file, name)`.\n   RESULT: `Array.iterator()` is a real `ArrayIterator` for the FIRST time\n   (was `ret $0` for as long as the wrap existed); in a map-using program all\n   three `MapKeyValueIterator` constructions resolve (g4a census: 0 wraps).\n\n**Residuals, precisely:**\n- `map.keyValueIterator()` explicit call still SIGILLs (132): the iterator's\n  ctor calls `map.keys()`, a BODYLESS declaration — none of keys/iterator/\n  keyValueIterator are in `runtime_mapping.rs` for the three maps (only\n  new/set/get/exists/remove/clear/toString/copy). Fix: mapping entries\n  `keysArray`/`valuesArray` → `haxe_*_keys_to_array`/`values_to_array`\n  (`returns: primitive`, like `String.split`) + bodies\n  `keys() return keysArray().iterator()` etc. — viable now that\n  `Array.iterator()` is real.\n- `StringTools` (28) still lowers BEFORE its iterators (30/31/33) although it\n  is a true 2-cycle (each iterator calls `StringTools.unsafeCodeAt`). The\n  tie-break should have favoured the iterators, so the `StringTools → iterator`\n  edge is probably MISSING from the graph, not mis-tied. Needs a graph dump.\n- hello world's maps still wrap 3× (they lower at 10/12/16, before the iterator\n  at 29) — a hello-world-only path; the user-facing map program is correct.\n- `Issue12700` became NONDETERMINISTIC (2/3 PASS on one binary; anon-struct\n  field named `length` written then read) — smells like uninitialised memory.\n\nConformance: 433 held, nothing lost; 4 CRASH→WRONG_ANSWER, 1 panic→SIGSEGV,\nIssue4675 flake. Attribution of the moves: A/B on two builds — discovery+meta alone leaves all six CRASH; adding the tie-break (cb5ee997) moves the four to WRONG_ANSWER; Issue3711 panic→SIGSEGV is the discovery commit (23e37cbd); Issue4675 is the flake..\n\n\n`for_in.rs:180-196` falls through to the array lowering when the operand's\nstatic type is Unknown, silently emitting an out-of-bounds strided read.\n**Non-local wrongness**: what `htmlEscape` returns depends on whether some\nunrelated part of the user's program mentions `haxe.iterators.StringIteratorUnicode` —\nif it does, `class_type` moves from TypeId(6)=Unknown to TypeId(471)=Class and\nthe same call returns EMPTY instead of 12x-length U+FFFD garbage. So D3 selects\nWHICH failure; D2 is why it fails at all.\nCheap defensive fix: refuse the array fallthrough on `TypeKind::Unknown`.\n\n\nNot bare-name binding. `haxe.ds.{StringMap,IntMap,ObjectMap}` are `extern class`\nwith bodyless `keys()`/`iterator()`/`keyValueIterator()`; `runtime_mapping.rs`\nbinds new/set/get/exists/remove/clear/toString/copy — NOT those three. A direct\n`m.keys()` dispatches into nothing (SIGILL 132); `for (k in m)` works only because\n`for_in.rs` calls `haxe_*_keys_to_array` itself. `keyValueIterator()`'s `#if eval`\nbody constructs `MapKeyValueIterator`, whose ctor calls `map.keys()` -\u003e same trap.\n\n**Correct fix (compiler/runtime, NOT .hx — user directive):** `map_method!`\nentries `\"keys\"`/`\"iterator\"` as `mir_wrapper`s that allocate a real\n`ArrayIterator` over `haxe_*_keys_to_array`/`values_to_array`, mirroring\n`Array.iterator =\u003e array_iterator` (runtime_mapping.rs:1913). PLUS the handle\ntyping fix below, or `next()` still yields an untyped i64.\n\n**Measured dead ends (do not repeat):** stdlib bodies\n`keys() return keysArray().iterator()` -\u003e `.iterator()` is intercepted by the\nmapping to the `array_iterator` intrinsic; `hasNext` true, `next()` returns the\nRIGHT pointer but typed i64 (prints `43622829024`, and `m.get(k)` SIGSEGVs).\nBodies `return new haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator(keysArray())` -\u003e loud SIGABRT\n\"`haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator.next` was never compiled\" (generic template not\ninstantiated for an in-stdlib construction). Both reverted; also non-upstream.\n\n\nFinal battery: 434/1240 vs 433 baseline; GAINED Issue3762, Issue4050 (both\nCOMPILE_FAIL -\u003e PASS); the single LOST row, Issue6480 `2 != 0`, is the recorded\nflake (5/5 PASS on the same binary in a mini-battery). Exposure, not regression\n(each confirmed on a HEAD build): Issue6457/6833 COMPILE_FAIL -\u003e stack overflow\n(D11), TestKeyValueIterator/TestXML COMPILE_FAIL -\u003e CRASH (MacroStringTools\nimport error exists at HEAD; Xml phi/return defects), Issue3462 compiler PANIC -\u003e\nloud FAILCHECK. Issue7672 and Issue3637 sit at their exact baseline shapes.\nThe stdlib harness baselines were re-recorded (6 FIXED -\u003e 'PASS'; g2a/g2d/g4a/g4b\nto their attributed shapes).\n\nProbes `mapkeys2` (`hasNext`/`next` on `m.keys()`), `mi_a`..`mi_g` (for-in over\n`keys()`/`iterator()` on StringMap/IntMap/`Map` abstract, Int and String elements)\nMATCH the oracle. None of the three works alone:\n\n1. **MIR — the handle is built at the stdlib-call return** (`iter_handle.rs`\n   `wrap_stdlib_iter_result`, called after the four sites that record the\n   return-class hint: `stdlib_instance.rs`, `extern_class.rs`, `stdlib_runtime.rs`\n   x2). Both consumers of a protocol-typed value tag-check it as a handle and\n   read a raw `ArrayIterator` as EXHAUSTED (`hasNext=false`, zero iterations).\n   At the extern-class site the raw result also fell into the auto-unbox that\n   resolves `T` from the receiver: `StringMap\u003cInt\u003e.keys()` -\u003e pointer through\n   `haxe_unbox_int_ptr` -\u003e truncated i32. The wrap returns BEFORE that.\n   `iter_elem_ir_type` must read `type_args` off the `TypeAlias` NODE\n   (`Iterator\u003cString\u003e` carries its argument there, the anonymous target does not).\n2. **stdlib bindings** (`compiler/src/stdlib/map_iter.rs`, new; `runtime_mapping.rs`\n   6 `map_method!` + 6 RETURN_CLASS rows): `{stringmap,intmap,objectmap}_{keys,values}_iterator`\n   = `array_iterator(haxe_*_to_array(map))`. `.hx` untouched (user directive).\n3. **tast — element typing** (`calls.rs` `structural_method_return_type` +\n   `alias_bindings`/`substitute_alias_args`; `loops.rs`\n   `infer_element_type_from_iterable` -\u003e `structural_element_type`; `NeedTypeAlias`\n   in `compute_type_substitution`). Without it `k` is `Dynamic`, MIR converts the\n   key to `*void`, and `k + \";\"` / `k.length` / `m.get(k)` deref an integer.\n   TRAP: `Iterator\u003cInt\u003e` is a **`Class { symbol_id: Iterator, type_args: [Int] }`\n   node**, not a `TypeAlias` node — the typedef was pre-registered as a class and\n   keeps that symbol kind. Bindings must be collected on the Class arm too; the\n   first attempt only bound on the TypeAlias arm and changed NOTHING\n   (`RAYZOR_STRUCT_TRACE=1` shows `class -\u003e alias`). See\n   [[bugs-structural-typedefs-are-class-symbols]].\n\n**Debugging that paid:** `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1` + `grep \"^fn @Main.main\"` and read the\nloop body (`bitcast $x to *void` on the element = typer gave Dynamic);\n`RAYZOR_STRUCT_TRACE=1` for the structural walk. My probe runner's exit code was\nBOGUS (`pipestatus` after `$(...)`): two SIGSEGVs printed as `exit=0`. Capture the\nstatus INSIDE the substitution (`raw=$(cmd; echo \"@@rc=$?\")`).\n\n**Side effect to attribute (HEAD baseline pending):** harness g2d\n(`for (c in StringTools.iterator(\"abc\"))`) flipped JITPANIC `can't resolve symbol\niterator` -\u003e `sum=0`. Mechanism: `stdlib_runtime.rs:914` infers a class from the\nRETURN type and falls back to `find_unique_by_method(\"iterator\")`; `Array.iterator`\nwas the ONLY entry, so a static inline `StringTools.iterator` (which has a BODY)\nwas bound to it by bare name. Six more `iterator` entries made the name ambiguous,\nthe fallback declines, and the remaining `sum=0` is the G2 defect (for-in over a\nhaxe-std iterator class yields nothing — same as `htmlEscape` returning \"\").\n\n**REGRESSION CAUGHT BEFORE COMMIT (battery 435/433, 0 lost, but 8 MOVED):** the\nstructural element typing gave `for (x in it)` inside `Lambda.fold\u003cA,B\u003e` the bare\nparameter `A` where it used to get `Dynamic`. `cranelift_backend.rs\nreferences_type_params` stubs a generic function whose LOCALS carry a `TypeVar`\n(`[trap-stub:generic] Lambda.fold`), and `main` cascades into a trap\n(`[trap-stub:cascade]`). `HelperMacros.hx` imports `haxe.macro.MacroStringTools`,\nwhose `toFieldExpr` calls `Lambda.fold` — so Issue7672/TestKeyValueIterator flipped\nto `error[IMPORT] ... unsupported expression type` (the `macro $i{s}` reification,\n`TypedExpressionKind::MacroExpression`, has no HIR lowering; it only became\nreachable). FIX: a bare `TypeParameter`/`Placeholder` element stays `Dynamic`\n(`is_bare_type_param` in loops.rs). PROBES: `mp5b` (Lambda.count/map/fold(0)),\n`mp3` (fold with `null` seed), `msf` (import MacroStringTools).\nHEAD comparison (stash -\u003e build -\u003e probe -\u003e pop): the MacroStringTools import\nerror is IDENTICAL at HEAD (`msf`), `Lambda.map`/`fold` already SIGSEGV at HEAD\n(only `count` worked, `mp5b`), `mp3` was already a silent wrong answer — so the\nonly true regression was the template-stub cascade; the rest is exposure.\nTOOL: `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` prints `[trap-stub:generic]`/`[trap-stub:cascade]`\nwith the function NAMES — the \"was never compiled\" banner alone names nothing.\n\n**g2d ROOT (2026-09-04):** `fn @StringTools.iterator($0: string) -\u003e *void { ret $0 }`\n— the `new StringIterator(s)` INSIDE haxe-std is value-wrapped (the D2 residual\nthat de609a53's gates were measured inert on); the for-in then calls the real\n`haxe.iterators.StringIterator.hasNext/next` on a STRING → false → `sum=0`.\n`RAYZOR_CTOR_DEBUG=1` also shows `VALUE-WRAP` for `StringIteratorUnicode` (g2a/g2b:\n`htmlEscape` returns \"\") and `MapKeyValueIterator` x3 (g4a/g4b exit 132). ONE\nresidual behind all six G2/G4 rows. Import order is NOT the cause here\n(StringIterator@27 \u003c StringTools@38). Next unit.\n\n2026-09-05 shape change after D12: ov1 now SIGSEGV (closure call through garbage in a user\n`KeyValueIterator` over `Map\u003cK,V\u003e`), ov2 now a BACKEND PANIC `llvm_jit_backend.rs:5129 Found\nIntValue i32 0 but expected PointerValue` at `PtrAdd` — no longer a TIMEOUT. Still `new Map()` with\nno type args; unchanged root.\n\n`var m = new Map(); m.set(24,124)` (type inferred later) and `Map\u003cK,V\u003e` inside a\ngeneric class both leave the `@:multiType` unresolved; calls then land on\n`fn @haxe.ds.Map.keys/set/remove/toString` (the abstract's own bodies), which call\n`this.keys()` on themselves -\u003e \"thread 'main' has overflowed its stack\" at RUN\ntime (MIR dump completes). Issue6457 and Issue6833 (COMPILE_FAIL -\u003e CRASH once\nthey compile). Probes `ov2`/`ov1`. Pre-existing runtime behaviour, newly reachable.\n\n**nue smoke (2026-09-04, at 59d5f337):** `rayzor run --llvm --release` on\nnue/examples/llama-chat compiles the whole nue graph and stops at its own usage\nline (no model) — clean. `enum-payload-smoke` runs and matches. OBSERVATION, not\nchased: `rayzor compile examples/llama-chat/Main.hx` (from nue/ or the example dir)\nfails `E0100 Cannot find type 'nue.transformer.GQAttention'` while `run` on the\nsame file does not — a class-path quirk of the `compile` subcommand, unknown\nwhether pre-existing. Use `run`, not `compile`, as the nue smoke.\n\n\n`new haxe.iterators.MapKeyValueIterator(this)` (the maps' `keyValueIterator()`)\nnow constructs (a3a08fd1), but its ctor's `map.keys()` with `map: IMap\u003cK,V\u003e` calls\n`IMap.keys` — \"was never compiled\" trap (exit 134). Interface vtable slots are\nfilled only for methods present in `function_map`; StringMap/IntMap/ObjectMap's\n`keys`/`iterator`/`get`... are runtime mappings (`map_method!`), so the IMap\nslots for them stay unfilled and dispatch falls to the declaration. Fix belongs\nwhere interface vtables are populated: fill a slot from the stdlib mapping's MIR\nwrapper (`stringmap_keys_iterator` etc.) when the implementing class is a mapped\nextern. Probes: harness g4a/g4b.\n\n\n`[20,10,30].sort(Reflect.compare)` stays `20,10,30` for `Array\u003cInt\u003e`, `Array\u003cString\u003e`\nAND `Array\u003cDynamic\u003e`; `sort(function(x,y) return x - y)` sorts. Probe `mi_h`.\nMECHANISM (2026-09-05, MIR): NOT a comparator-ABI problem — `a.sort(Reflect.compare)`\nemits NO `array_sort` call at all. `Reflect.compare\u003cT\u003e(a:T,b:T):Int` is a BODYLESS\ndeclaration in haxe-std (mapped to `haxe_reflect_compare(*u8,*u8)-\u003ei64`); taken as a\nVALUE it fails to lower, the argument fails, and the call (or the whole\n`var f = Reflect.compare; ... f(1,2)` statement) is DROPPED silently. User static refs\n(`a.sort(Main.cmp)`) lower fine (make_closure). Fix = synthesize a closure-ABI thunk\nfor a mapped static taken as a value: erased i64 args -\u003e the reference's instantiated\nparam types (`expr.ty` Function([Int,Int])) -\u003e boxed for the runtime's Dynamic formals\n-\u003e call -\u003e ret. Second defect: an argument that fails to lower must not drop the call\nsilently (indirect.rs already abandons loudly; the extern/array paths filter_map). Oracle-based tests that sort map keys\nfor determinism all read as order bugs because of this.\n\n**D9 FIX:** (tast) `instantiate_function_reference` in calls.rs — a function-typed\nargument whose params are still TypeParameters takes the expected arg type when it\nis a concrete Function of the same arity (lambdas excluded; they have their own\nhint). (mir) `mapped_static_function_ref` in decl/vtables.rs — a bodyless mapped\nstatic taken as a value gets a synthesised env-less function shaped like the\nreference's type: params boxed via `maybe_box_value(.., Dynamic)`, an erased param\nboxed as int (bit-faithful fallback for `var f = Reflect.compare`), the runtime\nextern called, return reconciled; deduped by name; tried in variables.rs after\n`get_function_id` misses. `haxe_array_sort(arr, fn_ptr, env)` calls\n`callback(env, a, b)`; a `fn_ref` of an env-less function works (user statics do),\nso the thunk mirrors a user static. Probes d9a/d9b/mi_h MATCH; `mapiter4`'s\n\"order\" diffs were this no-op all along. Battery flat at 439 (flakes only).\n\n\n`s += sm.get(\"a\")` on a `StringMap\u003cInt\u003e` SIGSEGVs (probe `mi_i1`, LITERAL key, so\nindependent of D4); `var v = sm.get(\"a\"); s += v` works (`mi_i3`, `mi_i5`). MIR:\n`$83 = call haxe_stringmap_get; cast u64-\u003ei64; bitcast -\u003e*u8; call\nhaxe_unbox_int_ptr` — the raw u64 is read as a boxed `Null\u003cInt\u003e` pointer. Same\nfamily as [[bugs-map-string-get-returns-raw-pointer-in-concat]] (inline use of a\n`get()` result mis-reads its representation; binding to a local first is the\nworkaround). Blocks `for (k in m.keys()) s += m.get(k)` (`mi_i4`).\n\n**D10 MECHANISM (MIR, 2026-09-05):** inline `s += m.get(\"a\")` -\u003e `call get -\u003e cast\nu64-\u003ei64 -\u003e bitcast -\u003e*u8 -\u003e haxe_unbox_int_ptr` (deref of the value = SIGSEGV);\n`var v = m.get(\"a\")` -\u003e `haxe_box_int_ptr(cast u64-\u003ei64)` at the initialiser, then\nthe same unbox = correct. Probe `mi_j` shows the two representations disagree on\nNULL: inline `m.get(\"zz\") == null` true (raw 0) but `m.get(\"a\") == null` for a\nstored 0 ALSO true (wrong); via a local, missing is NOT null (boxed 0) (wrong).\nFIXED 1d3e09fb: at BOTH stdlib return sites (`extern_class.rs` extern branch — the one `m.get` takes — and `stdlib_runtime.rs`) a declared `Null\u003cboxable scalar\u003e` is boxed by the raw\nregister's kind, null when the class's `exists` row (same arity) says absent —\n`box_raw_optional_result` in boxing.rs. A register-type heuristic in\n`maybe_unbox_optional` was REJECTED: an `Array\u003cNull\u003cInt\u003e\u003e` element arrives as a\npointer in an I64 register and would be misread as raw.\n\n**D10 second half (2026-09-05):** boxing the map result exposed a PRE-EXISTING gap:\na `Null\u003cscalar\u003e` BOX passed into a generic `T` formal travels as its ADDRESS\n(`eq\u003cT\u003e(20, v)` with `var v:Null\u003cInt\u003e = 20` printed `false b=53601562896` — probe\n`mi_k`), so Issue9026 (`eq(20, map.get(key))`) had passed only by the raw-value\nluck. Fix 1d3e09fb (in-module, `helpers/signatures.rs`) + 73a5f8e0 (cross-module IR-typed formals) + 8ffd3a8c (virtual dispatch + resolved_method parent-chain/field paths; local-callee signatures): a boxed\n`Null\u003cInt|Float|Bool\u003e` bound for a `TypeParameter` formal is unboxed\n(`maybe_unbox_optional`) and widened (`coerce_to_i64`) — the inverse of the\nexisting \"raw scalar into Dynamic/Null formal gets boxed\" rule. Null -\u003e 0 there\n(erasure; `haxe_unbox_int_ptr` is null-safe), pre-existing limit.\n\n**D10 battery (1d3e09fb):** 433 vs 435 = Issue6480 (flake) + Issue9026 EXPOSED, not\nregressed: its `var key = {}` is an empty BLOCK with no value\n([[bugs-empty-object-literal-is-parsed-as-a-block]]) — the MIR has NO objectmap_set\ncalls and `map.get(key)` is `const 0`; with a real key (`{x:1}`, probe `mi_k3`)\nObjectMap get -\u003e generic T MATCHES. Its earlier PASS was luck of the raw value.\n\n**Map iteration ORDER diverges:** rayzor's StringMap/IntMap iterate in hash order\n(`two;one`), `haxe --interp` in insertion order (`one;two`). Unspecified upstream;\noracle-based tests that print unsorted keys/values will DIFF for that reason\nalone — and sorting them hits D9. Compare as sets or sums.\n\n\nExplicit `m.keyValueIterator()` on `haxe.ds.StringMap`/`IntMap` SIGSEGVs (139);\n`for (k =\u003e v in m)` is FINE.\n**RE-ATTRIBUTION (inferred, not yet probed):** `eval` IS defined for rayzor, so\nthe `#if eval` branch is active and its body is `new haxe.iterators.MapKeyValueIterator(this)`\n— a ONE-ARG `new` of a stdlib class INSIDE haxe-std, i.e. D2's value wrap. The\nmap is handed